        <div class="flex-1 overflow-y-auto px-4 pb-4">
            <div class="border-t border-slate-800 pt-4 mt-2">
                <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
                    <h3 class="text-xs font-bold text-slate-400 uppercase tracking-wider">Memory Vault</h3>
                    <div class="flex items-center gap-1.5">
                        <button id="vaultUploadBtn" title="Upload file to vault" class="text-[10px] font-semibold px-2 py-1 rounded bg-slate-700 hover:bg-slate-600 text-slate-300 transition-colors">Upload</button>
                        <button id="vaultClearBtn" title="Clear all vault items" class="text-[10px] font-semibold px-2 py-1 rounded bg-red-900/50 hover:bg-red-800/60 text-red-400 transition-colors">Clear</button>
                        <input type="file" id="vaultUploadInput" class="hidden" multiple>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <ul id="sidebarStoreList" class="space-y-2">
                    <li class="text-slate-500 italic text-xs px-2">Vault is empty</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <button onclick="switchTab('localTab')" class="p-4 text-left w-full border-t border-slate-800 shrink-0 bg-slate-900 group hover:bg-slate-800/60 transition-colors">
            <span class="flex items-center gap-1.5 text-xs text-emerald-500 group-hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors">
                <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-3.5 w-3.5 shrink-0" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z" /></svg>
                100% Local &mdash; How it works
            </span>
            <span class="block text-[10px] text-slate-600 mt-0.5">Powered by WebAssembly OpenSSL</span>
        </button>
    </div>

    <div class="flex-1 overflow-y-auto p-8 relative">

        <div id="localTab" class="max-w-3xl mx-auto hidden pb-12">
            <div class="mb-8">
                <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-3">
                    <div class="p-2 rounded-lg bg-emerald-100">
                        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-5 w-5 text-emerald-600" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z" /></svg>
                    </div>
                    <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-slate-800">Why 100% Local?</h2>
                </div>
                <p class="text-slate-500 leading-relaxed">Every certificate operation in this toolkit runs entirely inside your browser. Nothing is ever sent to a server.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-emerald-50 border border-emerald-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-6">
                <h3 class="font-bold text-emerald-800 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
                    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-4 w-4" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z" /></svg>
                    TL;DR
                </h3>
                <ul class="space-y-2.5 text-sm text-emerald-900">
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500 font-bold mt-0.5 shrink-0">&#10003;</span><span>Your certificate files, private keys, and passwords <strong class="font-semibold">never leave your device</strong> &mdash; there is no backend server receiving them.</span></li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500 font-bold mt-0.5 shrink-0">&#10003;</span><span>All cryptographic operations (decryption, parsing, conversion) are performed by <strong class="font-semibold">OpenSSL running directly in your browser tab</strong>.</span></li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500 font-bold mt-0.5 shrink-0">&#10003;</span><span>The Memory Vault exists only in your browser&rsquo;s RAM for the current session &mdash; <strong class="font-semibold">nothing is written to disk or localStorage</strong>.</span></li>
                    <li class="flex items-start gap-2"><span class="text-emerald-500 font-bold mt-0.5 shrink-0">&#10003;</span><span>You can <strong class="font-semibold">download the page and run it completely offline</strong> &mdash; once the assets are loaded, no network connection is needed.</span></li>
                </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="space-y-5">

                <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
                    <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-800 mb-1">What is WebAssembly?</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">WebAssembly (WASM) is a low-level binary instruction format that runs inside the browser at near-native speed. Every modern browser (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge) includes a sandboxed WASM runtime as part of the JavaScript engine. Code compiled to WASM has <strong>no direct access to the operating system, the file system, or the network</strong> &mdash; it can only interact with what the JavaScript host explicitly provides to it.</p>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">This means a WASM module cannot exfiltrate data even if it wanted to: the sandbox physically prevents it from opening sockets or reading files outside the controlled API surface.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
                    <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-800 mb-1">OpenSSL compiled to WASM</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-3">OpenSSL is the industry-standard C library used by servers worldwide for TLS, certificate management, and cryptographic operations. Using Emscripten (a C/C++ to WebAssembly compiler toolchain), the OpenSSL source is compiled into a <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">openssl.wasm</code> binary and a thin JavaScript glue layer (<code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">openssl.js</code>).</p>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">The result is the <em>exact same</em> OpenSSL you would invoke from a terminal &mdash; <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">openssl x509</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">openssl pkcs12</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">openssl pkey</code> &mdash; executing inside a browser tab instead of a shell.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
                    <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-800 mb-1">How a tool operation works end-to-end</h3>
                    <ol class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed space-y-2 list-none">
                        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 text-slate-500 px-2 py-0.5 rounded h-max mt-0.5 shrink-0">1</span><span>Your file or pasted PEM data is read by JavaScript using the browser&rsquo;s <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">FileReader</code> API &mdash; it stays in the tab&rsquo;s memory.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 text-slate-500 px-2 py-0.5 rounded h-max mt-0.5 shrink-0">2</span><span>A WASM instance of <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">openssl.wasm</code> is initialised. Each operation typically gets its own isolated instance to avoid state bleed.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 text-slate-500 px-2 py-0.5 rounded h-max mt-0.5 shrink-0">3</span><span>The file bytes are written into the WASM module&rsquo;s linear memory (a private, sandboxed block of bytes &mdash; like RAM for the module). The OpenSSL command is then executed inside that memory space.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 text-slate-500 px-2 py-0.5 rounded h-max mt-0.5 shrink-0">4</span><span>OpenSSL writes its output (the parsed certificate fields, the converted file bytes, the extracted key) back to the same linear memory.</span></li>
                        <li class="flex gap-3"><span class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 text-slate-500 px-2 py-0.5 rounded h-max mt-0.5 shrink-0">5</span><span>JavaScript reads the result out and renders it in the UI or offers it as a download via a temporary <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">Blob URL</code>. The WASM instance is then discarded.</span></li>
                    </ol>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 leading-relaxed mt-3">At no point in this flow is there a <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">fetch()</code> or <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">XMLHttpRequest</code> carrying your data. You can verify this yourself by opening DevTools &#8594; Network and filtering by XHR/Fetch while using any tool.</p>
                </div>

                <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
                    <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-800 mb-1">Memory Vault &amp; session scope</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-slate-600 leading-relaxed">The Memory Vault is a plain JavaScript array held in the page&rsquo;s runtime memory. It is never written to <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">localStorage</code>, <code class="font-mono text-xs bg-slate-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">sessionStorage</code>, IndexedDB, or cookies. Closing or refreshing the tab immediately discards all vault contents. This is intentional &mdash; private keys should not persist beyond the session you need them.</p>
                </div>

            </div>

            <p class="mt-8 text-center text-xs text-slate-400">You can audit the full source at any time &mdash; open DevTools and inspect <code class="font-mono bg-slate-100 px-1 rounded">js/</code>.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="homeTab" class="max-w-4xl mx-auto block">
            <div class="mb-10 text-center">
                <div class="flex justify-center mb-5">
                    <div class="p-4 rounded-2xl bg-blue-600/10 border border-blue-200">
                        <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="h-12 w-12 text-blue-600" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                            <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1.5" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4m5.618-4.016A11.955 11.955 0 0112 2.944a11.955 11.955 0 01-8.618 3.04A12.02 12.02 0 003 9c0 5.591 3.824 10.29 9 11.622 5.176-1.332 9-6.03 9-11.622 0-1.042-.133-2.052-.382-3.016z" />
                        </svg>
                    </div>
                </div>
                <h2 class="text-4xl font-bold text-slate-800 mb-3">TestingServers.com Toolkit</h2>
                <p class="text-slate-500 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">Browser-based PKI, security, developer, and network tools. Client-side operations run 100% locally via WebAssembly. Network tools use a lightweight API proxy.</p>
            </div>

